+# Guix System on the LG gram 16 2-in-1 (16T90R): install steps
+
+This replaces Fedora on the gram. **The install erases the internal disk.**
+Do section A on Fedora first.
+
+Files in this folder:
+
+- `config.scm`: the system (kernel, Wi-Fi and sound firmware, disk, user
+  calmy, Bluetooth, tuigreet login screen, zram, power profiles, Tailscale).
+- `home.scm`: your programs and dotfiles (Hyprland, Waybar, swaync quick
+  settings, night light, LibreOffice, Krita, Kdenlive, mpv, Electrum, Tor
+  Browser, Wine, Steam, Flatpak for Zen, Neovim tools).
+- `installer.scm`: the Guix installer with the full kernel from nonguix,
+  so that the gram's Wi-Fi works in the installer. You build it on the
+  Panasonic (A4).
+- `../channels.scm`: Guix + nonguix + tailscale. The same file as for the
+  Panasonic. Used only for the first install.
+
+Differences from the Panasonic steps (`../INSTALL.md`):
+
+- The gram has no Ethernet port, and the normal Guix installer has no
+  driver for the gram's Wi-Fi. So you use a nonguix installer stick (A4),
+  and connect to Wi-Fi in the installer (C).
+- The disk is NVMe: the partitions are `nvme0n1p1` and `nvme0n1p2`.
+- No swap file. zram (in config.scm) does the same as on Fedora.
+- You do not copy or edit configs by hand. home.scm links `~/.config/hypr`,
+  `waybar`, `fuzzel`, ... to `~/dots`, and `hypr/hyprland.lua` in the
+  repository is already changed for Guix.
+
+
+## A. On Fedora, before you erase
+
+### A1. Put the new files in the repository
+
+    cd ~/dots
+    git am ~/Downloads/0001-guix-gram-config-for-the-LG-gram.patch
+    git push
+
+(Change the path to where you saved the patch.)
+
+### A2. Optional: more files for the repository
+
+- **qt6ct** (the look of Krita, Kdenlive and other Qt programs). home.scm
+  links `~/.config/qt6ct` if the folder is in the repository:
+
+      cp -r ~/.config/qt6ct ~/dots/
+      grep -n /usr ~/dots/qt6ct/qt6ct.conf
+
+  If grep shows a line with `/usr/share/qt6ct/...`, change `/usr` to
+  `/home/calmy/.guix-home/profile` in that line. Guix has no `/usr/share`.
+
+- **Scripts from `~/.local/bin`** that you want on the gram (for example
+  `cast-start`, `cast-stop`, `tvplay`, `tv-audio`). Every file in `bin/`
+  becomes a link in `~/.local/bin`:
+
+      cp ~/.local/bin/{cast-start,cast-stop,tvplay,tv-audio} ~/dots/bin/
+
+  These scripts can use Fedora programs. Send them to me if you want me to
+  check them.
+
+Then commit and push:
+
+    cd ~/dots && git add -A && git commit -m "qt6ct and scripts" && git push
+
+### A3. Back up what is not on the flash drive yet
+
+The flash drive has Documents, Projects and .zen. Do these steps too.
+
+1. Make sure that the archives on the flash drive are complete. Each line
+   must start with `OK`:
+
+       M=/run/media/calmy/main
+       for f in "$M"/*.tar.gz "$M"/.zen.tar.gz; do
+         tar -tzf "$f" >/dev/null && echo "OK  $f" || echo "BAD $f"
+       done
+
+2. Make sure that `.zen.tar.gz` has your real Zen profile. In Zen, open
+   `about:profiles`. Look at the "Root Directory" of the profile that is in
+   use.
+   - If it is in `~/.zen`, the archive is correct. If you used Zen after
+     you made it (31 Oct, 00:09), close Zen and make it again:
+
+         tar -czf "$M/.zen.tar.gz" -C ~ .zen
+
+   - If it is in `~/.config/zen`, the archive does not have your profile.
+     Close Zen and make it from that folder:
+
+         tar -czf "$M/.zen.tar.gz" -C ~/.config zen
+
+   Section G finds the profile in either archive.
+
+3. **CAUTION: your Bitcoin wallet is in `~/.electrum`. It is not on the
+   flash drive yet.** Make sure that you have the seed words on paper
+   (Electrum: Wallet > Seed). Then back up the wallet files with the other
+   small things (SSH keys, GPG keys, git settings, your scripts):
+
+       tar --ignore-failed-read -czf "$M/small-stuff.tar.gz" -C ~ \
+         .electrum .ssh .gnupg .gitconfig .local/bin .config/qt6ct
+       tar -tzf "$M/small-stuff.tar.gz" | cut -d/ -f1 | sort -u
+
+   The last command must show `.electrum` and `.ssh`.
+
+4. Look for other big folders that are not backed up (Pictures, Downloads,
+   Videos, a Wine folder `~/.wine`, ...):
+
+       du -sh ~/* ~/.[!.]* 2>/dev/null | sort -h | tail -25
+
+   Back up the ones that you need in the same way. The flash drive is FAT32:
+   one file can be 4 GiB maximum.
+
+5. Not necessary: Steam games (they download again), Bitwarden, Telegram
+   (they are in the cloud), Wi-Fi passwords (you type them again).
+
+### A4. Make the nonguix installer stick (on the Panasonic)
+
+The gram has no Ethernet port. Your Guix installer stick has no driver for
+the gram's Wi-Fi. `installer.scm` makes a Guix installer with the full
+kernel and firmware from nonguix. The Panasonic already runs that kernel,
+so it builds the image without compiling.
+
+Write the image to your old Guix installer stick. Do not use the backup
+flash drive. Do A1 first, so that `installer.scm` is on GitHub.
+
+1. On the Panasonic, get the files into a new folder. (Your `~/dots` on
+   the Panasonic does not change.)
+
+       git clone https://github.com/CalMy-2112/dotfiles /tmp/dots
+
+   Do not run `guix pull` before the next steps. The Panasonic's present
+   Guix has the kernel and firmware in its store already. A newer Guix can
+   need a kernel that nonguix has not built yet. Then the kernel compiles,
+   and that takes many hours.
+
+2. Make sure that there is space. The image and its parts need about 6 GB:
+
+       df -h /gnu
+
+3. Build the image. This takes 10 to 40 minutes. It downloads the parts
+   that are not on the Panasonic yet:
+
+       ISO=$(guix system image --image-type=iso9660 /tmp/dots/guix/gram/installer.scm)
+       echo $ISO
+
+   The last line shows a file name like `/gnu/store/...-image.iso`.
+   If you see `building` and then a name with `-linux-6` or `-linux-7` in
+   it (the kernel), stop with Ctrl+C and tell me.
+
+4. Put in the old Guix installer stick. Find it, and compare its size with
+   the size of the image. The stick must be larger:
+
+       lsblk -o NAME,SIZE,MODEL,TRAN
+       ls -lh $ISO
+
+   **CAUTION: on the Panasonic, `sda` is the internal disk (TRAN = sata).**
+   The stick is the line with TRAN = usb, for example `sdb`. Change `sdX`
+   to it. dd erases the disk that you name.
+
+       sudo dd if=$ISO of=/dev/sdX bs=4M status=progress oflag=sync
+
+
+## B. Boot the installer
+
+1. Remove the backup flash drive. Put in the nonguix installer stick (A4).
+2. Start the gram. Press F2 at the LG logo to open the setup utility.
+   Find **Secure Boot** (usually on the Boot or Security page) and set it
+   to **Disabled**. Guix's boot loader is not signed. Save and exit.
+3. Press F10 at the LG logo. Select the USB stick.
+4. When the installer menu shows, press Ctrl+Alt+F3. You get a root shell.
+   The text is small on this screen. That is normal.
+5. Make sure that the gram booted in UEFI mode. This command must list
+   files:
+
+       ls /sys/firmware/efi
+
+
+## C. Network (Wi-Fi)
+
+1. Make sure that you booted the nonguix stick:
+
+       uname -r
+       ip -br link
+
+   `uname -r` must not end in `-gnu` (that is the normal Guix kernel, which
+   has no Wi-Fi driver for the gram). `ip -br link` must show a line that
+   starts with `wl`, for example `wlan0` or `wlp0s20f3`.
+
+2. Connect to your Wi-Fi:
+
+       connmanctl
+
+   Then type these commands in connmanctl, one at a time:
+
+       enable wifi
+       scan wifi
+       services
+       agent on
+       connect wifi_..._psk
+       quit
+
+   For `connect`, use the ID that `services` shows next to your network
+   name. connmanctl asks for the password.
+
+3. Make sure that the network works:
+
+       ping -c 3 gnu.org
+
+You can also connect on the network page of the installer menu (tty1), and
+then go back to the root shell with Ctrl+Alt+F3.
+
+
+## D. Disk
+
+**CAUTION: these commands erase Fedora and all files on the internal disk.**
+
+1. Find the internal disk. It is `nvme0n1` (953.9G):
+
+       lsblk -o NAME,SIZE,MODEL,TRAN
+
+2. Erase, partition and format:
+
+       DISK=/dev/nvme0n1
+       wipefs -a ${DISK}p1 ${DISK}p2 ${DISK}p3 $DISK
+       parted -s -a optimal $DISK mklabel gpt \
+         mkpart ESP fat32 1MiB 1025MiB set 1 esp on \
+         mkpart root ext4 1025MiB 100%
+       mkfs.fat -F32 -i 1234ABCD ${DISK}p1
+       mkfs.ext4 -L guix-root ${DISK}p2
+
+   config.scm finds the disks by the label `guix-root` and the ID
+   `1234-ABCD`. Do not change one without the other.
+
+3. Mount, and keep the installer's downloads on the disk:
+
+       mount LABEL=guix-root /mnt
+       mkdir -p /mnt/boot/efi
+       mount ${DISK}p1 /mnt/boot/efi
+       herd start cow-store /mnt
+
+   No swap file is necessary: the gram has 32 GB of memory.
+
+
+## E. Install
+
+1. Get your files:
+
+       guix shell git -- git clone https://github.com/CalMy-2112/dotfiles /mnt/root/dots
+       mkdir -p /mnt/etc
+       cp /mnt/root/dots/guix/gram/config.scm /mnt/etc/config.scm
+
+2. Trust the nonguix build server, so that the kernel and firmware
+   download ready-built. (The installer from A4 has the nonguix kernel, but
+   it does not trust the nonguix build server yet.)
+
+       wget -O /tmp/nonguix.pub https://substitutes.nonguix.org/signing-key.pub
+       cat /tmp/nonguix.pub
+       guix archive --authorize < /tmp/nonguix.pub
+
+   The `q #...#` value in the output must be the same as in config.scm
+   (C1FD53E5...EA98). If it is not, stop and tell me.
+
+3. Install. This takes 30 to 90 minutes:
+
+       mkdir -p /mnt/var/cache/install
+       export XDG_CACHE_HOME=/mnt/var/cache/install
+       guix time-machine -C /mnt/root/dots/guix/channels.scm -- \
+         system init /mnt/etc/config.scm /mnt \
+         --substitute-urls='https://ci.guix.gnu.org https://bordeaux.guix.gnu.org https://substitutes.nonguix.org'
+
+   If you see `building ...-linux-6...` or `...-linux-7...` (the kernel),
+   nonguix has no ready-built kernel for this Guix version yet. Compiling it
+   takes hours. You can let it run, or stop it with Ctrl+C and run the same
+   command again later. Tailscale always builds from source (some minutes).
+
+4. Restart and remove the USB stick:
+
+       reboot
+
+
+## F. First boot
+
+The tuigreet login screen shows on tty1. It starts Hyprland, and Hyprland
+is not installed until step 4. So do steps 1 to 4 on the text login on tty2.
+
+1. Press Ctrl+Alt+F2. Log in as `root`. The password is empty. Set the
+   passwords, then log out:
+
+       passwd
+       passwd calmy
+       exit
+
+2. Log in as `calmy` at the same prompt. Do not use `su calmy`:
+   NetworkManager (and later Hyprland) refuses, because you are not logged
+   in at the laptop. Wi-Fi now works:
+
+       nmcli device wifi connect "NETWORK NAME" --ask
+
+3. Update Guix. The first time takes 10 to 30 minutes.
+   /etc/guix/channels.scm already includes nonguix and tailscale.
+
+       guix pull
+       hash guix
+
+4. Get your files and install your programs. This downloads many GB
+   (LibreOffice, Kdenlive, Krita, Steam, Tor Browser), so it takes a long
+   time:
+
+       guix shell git -- git clone https://github.com/CalMy-2112/dotfiles ~/dots
+       guix home reconfigure ~/dots/guix/gram/home.scm
+
+   Read the first lines of the output:
+   - `home.scm: Hyprland 0.55.x (reads hyprland.lua)` is correct.
+   - `home.scm: Guix has no package "NAME"; skipped` means that one program
+     was not installed. The rest continues. Tell me the names.
+
+   Guix Home moves old files that it replaces to a backup folder in your
+   home. That is normal.
+
+5. Log out (`exit`). Press Ctrl+Alt+F1 and log in at tuigreet. Hyprland
+   starts. On tty2 (Ctrl+Alt+F2) you always get a text login. Use it if
+   Hyprland does not start.
+
+6. Delete the installer's download folder:
+
+       sudo rm -rf /var/cache/install
+
+
+## G. Restore your files
+
+1. Put in the backup flash drive. udiskie mounts it and shows a
+   notification. Find where:
+
+       lsblk -o NAME,SIZE,LABEL,MOUNTPOINTS
+
+   Set M to the mount point, for example:
+
+       M=/media/calmy/main
+
+2. Documents and Projects. First look at how the archive starts:
+
+       tar -tzf "$M/Documents.tar.gz" | head -3
+
+   - If the lines start with `Documents/`: `tar -xzf "$M/Documents.tar.gz" -C ~`
+   - If they start with `home/calmy/`: `tar -xzf "$M/Documents.tar.gz" -C /`
+
+   Do the same for `Projects.tar.gz`.
+
+3. Electrum wallet, SSH keys, GPG keys, git settings:
+
+       mkdir -p ~/restore
+       tar -xzf "$M/small-stuff.tar.gz" -C ~/restore
+       cp -a ~/restore/.electrum ~/restore/.ssh ~/
+       cp -a ~/restore/.gnupg ~/ 2>/dev/null
+       cp -a ~/restore/.gitconfig ~/ 2>/dev/null
+
+   Do not copy `~/restore/.local/bin` to `~/.local/bin`: Guix Home makes
+   that folder from `~/dots/bin`. The old scripts stay in
+   `~/restore/.local/bin` for you to look at.
+
+   Start `electrum`. It must show your wallet. Then you can delete
+   `~/restore`.
+
+4. Zen. Guix has no Zen package, so it comes from Flathub:
+
+       flatpak remote-add --user --if-not-exists flathub https://dl.flathub.org/repo/flathub.flatpakrepo
+       flatpak install --user flathub app.zen_browser.zen
+       flatpak run app.zen_browser.zen
+
+   Close Zen when the window shows. Now put your old profile in:
+
+       Z=~/.var/app/app.zen_browser.zen
+       ls -a $Z $Z/config 2>/dev/null
+       mkdir -p ~/restore-zen
+       tar -xzf "$M/.zen.tar.gz" -C ~/restore-zen
+       OLD=$(dirname "$(find ~/restore-zen -name profiles.ini | head -1)")
+       echo $OLD
+
+   The `ls` shows the folder that Zen made: `.zen` in $Z, or `zen` in
+   `$Z/config`. Set NEW to it, then replace it with your old one:
+
+       NEW=$Z/.zen            # or: NEW=$Z/config/zen
+       mv "$NEW" "$NEW.empty"
+       cp -a "$OLD" "$NEW"
+       flatpak run app.zen_browser.zen
+
+   If Zen shows no old tabs, open `about:profiles`, find your old profile,
+   click "Set as default profile" and start Zen again. When all is
+   correct, delete `~/restore-zen` and `$NEW.empty`.
+
+   Zen shows in fuzzel after the next login. To update Zen:
+   `flatpak update`.
+
+
+## H. Other programs
+
+- **Steam:** run `steam`. The first start downloads Steam itself. Steam
+  runs in a container. Games go in `~/.local/share/guix-sandbox-home`.
+- **Wine:** run `winecfg` once. It makes `~/.wine`. If you backed up an
+  old `~/.wine`, restore it before that. Guix has no winetricks package.
+- **Tor Browser:** the command is `torbrowser`.
+- **LibreOffice, Krita, Kdenlive, mpv, Electrum:** start them from fuzzel
+  (Super+D).
+- **Tailscale:** `sudo tailscale up`, then open the link that it shows.
+- **Neovim tools that are not in Guix** (pyright, bashls, ts_ls, asm-lsp,
+  swank, metals, molten): do steps F5 to F8 in `../INSTALL.md`. They are
+  the same on the gram.
+
+
+## I. Check the gram
+
+Do these checks once. Tell me which ones fail.
+
+1. **Speakers.** Play a video or song. You said that the speakers work on
+   Fedora (kernel 7.2.13), so the kernel has the fix for this model. If
+   there is no sound from the speakers:
+
+       uname -r
+       cat /proc/asound/cards
+
+2. **Quick settings.** Super+S opens the swaync panel. Try Wi-Fi,
+   Bluetooth, Night Light, the sliders, Networks, Devices, Settings.
+   Also check:
+
+       swaync --version
+
+   Your style.css uses CSS variables (`--cc-bg`, ...). Only swaync 0.12 or
+   later reads them. If the version is older, the panel colors are partly
+   wrong. Tell me the version.
+
+3. **Night light.** Super+N, and the moon or sun icon in Waybar.
+4. **Brightness keys** and the keyboard backlight slider.
+5. **zram:** `swapon --show` shows `/dev/zram0` with 8G.
+6. **Power profiles:** `powerprofilesctl` shows `balanced`.
+7. **Touchscreen and pen:** draw in Krita.
+8. **Suspend:** close the lid, open it. hyprlock must ask for your
+   password.
+
+
+## J. If something does not work
+
+- **No Wi-Fi in the installer** (`uname -r` ends in `-gnu`, or no `wl...`
+  line): you booted the old Guix stick. Do A4 again, and check that dd
+  wrote to the stick.
+- **"No bootable device" after the install:** press F10 at the LG logo and
+  select the Guix entry. To remove Fedora's old boot entry:
+
+      sudo guix shell efibootmgr -- efibootmgr
+      sudo guix shell efibootmgr -- efibootmgr -b NNNN -B
+
+  (NNNN is the number of the Fedora line.)
+- **Hyprland does not start at tuigreet:** press Ctrl+Alt+F2, log in, and
+  run `start-hyprland` there. The error shows in the terminal.
+- **A swaync button does nothing:** run its command (in
+  `~/dots/swaync/config.json`) in a terminal. The error shows there.
+- **A key or a Waybar click does nothing:** the program is probably not
+  installed. Find it with `guix search NAME`, add it to home.scm and run
+  `guix home reconfigure` again.
+- To go back to an older system, select it in the GRUB menu at boot.
+  To undo the last home change: `guix home roll-back`.
+
+
+## Later
+
+    guix pull                                              # new package versions
+    sudo guix system reconfigure ~/dots/guix/gram/config.scm
+    guix home reconfigure ~/dots/guix/gram/home.scm
+    flatpak update                                         # Zen
+
+Your configs in `~/.config` (hypr, waybar, fuzzel, alacritty, fastfetch,
+yazi, helix, nvim, swaync/config.json) are links to `~/dots`. Edit them
+there, then commit and push from `~/dots`. Two exceptions:
+
+- `swaync/style.css` is a copy (see `%swaync-style` in home.scm). After you
+  edit `~/dots/swaync/style.css`, run `guix home reconfigure` again.
+- `.zshrc` and `.tmux.conf.local` are copies too. Run `guix home
+  reconfigure` after you edit them in `~/dots`.
+
+
+## Known differences from Fedora
+
+- Hyprland is the Guix version (0.55.x), not 0.57. hyprland.lua is for
+  0.55, so it works.
+- Zen is a Flatpak. It updates with `flatpak update`, not with Guix.
+- Steam runs in a container. Its games are in
+  `~/.local/share/guix-sandbox-home`, not in `~/.local/share/Steam`.
+- Not in this config (Fedora had them): winetricks (Guix has no package),
+  Docker, Telegram. Tell me if you want them.
+- No systemd. Use `loginctl` (suspend, reboot, poweroff) and
+  `sudo herd status` for services.
+- hyprpaper runs through a small wrapper in home.scm, the same as on the
+  Panasonic (a Guix EGL problem).
diff --git a/guix/gram/config.scm b/guix/gram/config.scm
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..6e23d63
--- /dev/null
+++ b/guix/gram/config.scm
@@ -0,0 +1,234 @@
+;; config.scm -- Guix System for the LG gram 16 2-in-1 (16T90R-K.ADB9U1)
+;;
+;; Made from ../config.scm (the Panasonic). Changes for the gram:
+;;   - NVMe disk (nvme0n1p1, nvme0n1p2), no swap file: zram, like Fedora
+;;   - US keyboard, no circscroll (the gram has a normal touchpad)
+;;   - user calmy, host calmySchool (the same as on Fedora)
+;;   - Sound Open Firmware (the gram's Intel audio needs it)
+;;   - power-profiles-daemon (Fedora had tuned-ppd)
+;;   - udev rules: Steam controllers, rfkill for the swaync Bluetooth button
+;;
+;; First install, from the live USB (see INSTALL.md in this folder):
+;;   guix time-machine -C ../channels.scm -- system init config.scm /mnt
+;; Later changes, on the installed system:
+;;   sudo guix system reconfigure ~/dots/guix/gram/config.scm
+;;
+;; Programs for your user are in home.scm, not here.
+
+(use-modules (gnu)
+             (guix channels)
+             (srfi srfi-1)                      ;find, remove
+             ((gnu packages admin) #:select (tuigreet))
+             ((gnu packages fonts) #:select (font-terminus))
+             ((gnu packages linux) #:select (brightnessctl))
+             ((gnu packages shells) #:select (zsh))
+             ((gnu packages xdisorg) #:select (hyprlock))
+             (nongnu packages linux)            ;full kernel and firmware
+             (nongnu system linux-initrd)       ;CPU microcode
+             (btv tailscale))                   ;tailscale channel
+(use-service-modules desktop linux pm xorg)
+
+;; nonguix: the full Linux kernel and the firmware for the Intel Wi-Fi,
+;; Bluetooth, GPU and audio. The same lines are in ../channels.scm.
+(define %nonguix-channel
+  (channel
+   (name 'nonguix)
+   (url "https://gitlab.com/nonguix/nonguix")
+   (introduction
+    (make-channel-introduction
+     "897c1a470da759236cc11798f4e0a5f7d4d59fbc"
+     (openpgp-fingerprint
+      "2A39 3FFF 68F4 EF7A 3D29  12AF 6F51 20A0 22FB B2D5")))))
+
+;; Tailscale (Fedora had it too). The same lines are in ../channels.scm.
+;; There is no build server: Guix builds it from source.
+(define %tailscale-channel
+  (channel
+   (name 'tailscale)
+   (url "https://github.com/umanwizard/guix-tailscale")
+   (branch "main")
+   (introduction
+    (make-channel-introduction
+     "52e82318f282c555757ef077d93a685fda97565d"
+     (openpgp-fingerprint
+      "9E53FC33B8328C745E7B31F70226C10D7877B741")))))
+
+;; Signing key of the nonguix build server. It must be the same as
+;; https://substitutes.nonguix.org/signing-key.pub
+(define %nonguix-key
+  (plain-file
+   "nonguix.pub"
+   "(public-key (ecc (curve Ed25519) (q #C1FD53E5D4CE971933EC50C9F307AE2171A2D3B52C804642A7A35F84F3A4EA98#)))"))
+
+;; Disks. INSTALL.md makes the partitions with this label and this ID.
+(define %root-fs
+  (file-system
+    (mount-point "/")
+    (device (file-system-label "guix-root"))
+    (type "ext4")))
+
+(define %esp-fs
+  (file-system
+    (mount-point "/boot/efi")
+    (device (uuid "1234-ABCD" 'fat))    ;made by: mkfs.fat -i 1234ABCD
+    (type "vfat")))
+
+;; Login screen: tuigreet, the same as on the Panasonic.
+;; After login it starts Hyprland.
+(define %tuigreet
+  (program-file
+   "tuigreet-session"
+   #~(execl #$(file-append tuigreet "/bin/tuigreet") "tuigreet"
+            "--time" "--remember" "--asterisks"
+            "--cmd" "start-hyprland")))
+
+;; tuigreet keeps the last user name in /var/cache/tuigreet ("--remember").
+;; The "greeter" user must be able to write there. This runs at every boot.
+(define %tuigreet-cache
+  (simple-service
+   'tuigreet-cache activation-service-type
+   (with-imported-modules '((guix build utils))
+     #~(begin
+         (use-modules (guix build utils))
+         (let ((greeter (false-if-exception (getpwnam "greeter"))))
+           (mkdir-p "/var/cache/tuigreet")
+           (when greeter
+             (chown "/var/cache/tuigreet"
+                    (passwd:uid greeter) (passwd:gid greeter))))))))
+
+;; Big console font for tuigreet and the text consoles (Terminus 16x32).
+;; The 16" 2560x1600 screen makes the normal 8x16 font very small.
+(define %console-fonts
+  (map (lambda (tty)
+         (cons tty (file-append font-terminus "/share/consolefonts/ter-132n")))
+       '("tty1" "tty2" "tty3" "tty4" "tty5" "tty6")))
+
+;; The Bluetooth button in the swaync panel runs "rfkill unblock bluetooth"
+;; as your user. Let the "netdev" group (you) use /dev/rfkill.
+(define %rfkill-rule
+  (udev-rule "90-rfkill-netdev.rules"
+             "KERNEL==\"rfkill\", GROUP=\"netdev\", MODE=\"0664\"\n"))
+
+;; power-profiles-daemon (balanced / power-saver / performance, also in
+;; GNOME Settings > Power). Added only if %desktop-services does not already
+;; have it, because a service type two times is an error.
+(define %power-profiles
+  (if (find (lambda (service)
+              (eq? (service-kind service) power-profiles-daemon-service-type))
+            %desktop-services)
+      '()
+      (list (service power-profiles-daemon-service-type))))
+
+;; tuigreet uses tty1, so remove the text login on tty1.
+;; tty2 to tty6 keep the normal text login (Ctrl+Alt+F2).
+(define (tty1-login? service)
+  (and (eq? (service-kind service) mingetty-service-type)
+       (string=? "tty1" (mingetty-configuration-tty (service-value service)))))
+
+(operating-system
+  (host-name "calmySchool")
+  (timezone "America/New_York")
+  (locale "en_US.utf8")
+
+  (keyboard-layout (keyboard-layout "us"))
+
+  (kernel linux)
+  (initrd microcode-initrd)
+  ;; sof-firmware: the gram's sound card (Intel Raptor Lake, SOF driver).
+  ;; Fedora has the same files in alsa-sof-firmware.
+  ;; specification->package finds it in whichever nonguix module has it.
+  (firmware (list linux-firmware
+                  (specification->package "sof-firmware")))
+
+  ;; Less text at boot: "quiet" is already in the defaults, loglevel=3 also
+  ;; hides kernel warnings.
+  (kernel-arguments (cons "loglevel=3" %default-kernel-arguments))
+
+  (bootloader (bootloader-configuration
+               (bootloader grub-efi-bootloader)
+               (targets '("/boot/efi"))
+               (keyboard-layout keyboard-layout)))
+
+  (file-systems (cons* %root-fs %esp-fs %base-file-systems))
+
+  (users (cons (user-account
+                (name "calmy")
+                (group "users")
+                (home-directory "/home/calmy")
+                (shell (file-append zsh "/bin/zsh"))
+                (supplementary-groups '("wheel" "netdev" "audio" "video")))
+               %base-user-accounts))
+
+  ;; tailscale: the "tailscale" command (the service only runs tailscaled).
+  (packages (cons* tailscale %base-packages))
+
+  (services
+   (append
+    (list
+     ;; Login screen on tty1.
+     (service greetd-service-type
+              (greetd-configuration
+               (terminals
+                (list (greetd-terminal-configuration
+                       (terminal-vt "1")
+                       (terminal-switch #t)
+                       (default-session-command %tuigreet))))))
+     %tuigreet-cache
+
+     ;; hyprlock checks your password with the PAM file /etc/pam.d/hyprlock.
+     ;; This service makes that file. (hyprlock itself is in home.scm.)
+     (service screen-locker-service-type
+              (screen-locker-configuration
+               (name "hyprlock")
+               (program (file-append hyprlock "/bin/hyprlock"))
+               (using-pam? #t)
+               (using-setuid? #f)))
+
+     ;; Bluetooth (Waybar module, swaync button, blueman).
+     (service bluetooth-service-type
+              (bluetooth-configuration (auto-enable? #t)))
+
+     ;; Brightness keys and the swaync slider without sudo ("video" group).
+     (udev-rules-service 'brightnessctl brightnessctl)
+
+     ;; Steam controllers and other game pads (Fedora: steam-devices).
+     (udev-rules-service 'steam-devices
+                         (specification->package "steam-devices-udev-rules"))
+
+     ;; swaync Bluetooth button (see %rfkill-rule above).
+     (udev-rules-service 'rfkill-netdev %rfkill-rule)
+
+     ;; Compressed swap in RAM, like Fedora's zram0 (8 GiB). No swap file.
+     (service zram-device-service-type
+              (zram-device-configuration
+               (size "8G")
+               (compression-algorithm 'zstd)))
+
+     ;; Tailscale daemon (Shepherd service "tailscaled"). State is in
+     ;; /var/lib/tailscale. Log in once with: sudo tailscale up
+     (service tailscale-service-type))
+
+    %power-profiles
+
+    ;; %desktop-services: NetworkManager, elogind, D-Bus, polkit, udisks...
+    (remove tty1-login?
+            (modify-services %desktop-services
+              ;; greetd replaces the GNOME login screen.
+              (delete gdm-service-type)
+              (console-font-service-type config => %console-fonts)
+              (guix-service-type
+               config => (guix-configuration
+                          (inherit config)
+                          ;; Write nonguix and tailscale into
+                          ;; /etc/guix/channels.scm, so that a plain
+                          ;; "guix pull" gets them.
+                          (channels (cons* %tailscale-channel
+                                           %nonguix-channel
+                                           %default-channels))
+                          ;; Download the kernel and firmware already built.
+                          (substitute-urls
+                           (append (guix-configuration-substitute-urls config)
+                                   (list "https://substitutes.nonguix.org")))
+                          (authorized-keys
+                           (append (guix-configuration-authorized-keys config)
+                                   (list %nonguix-key))))))))))
diff --git a/guix/gram/home.scm b/guix/gram/home.scm
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..ecaed04
--- /dev/null
+++ b/guix/gram/home.scm
@@ -0,0 +1,486 @@
+;; home.scm -- programs, dotfiles and user services for calmy on the
+;; LG gram (Guix Home)
+;;
+;;   guix home reconfigure ~/dots/guix/gram/home.scm
+;;
+;; Made from ../home.scm (the Panasonic). This file is in guix/gram/ of your
+;; dotfiles repository (~/dots). It uses these repository files, if they are
+;; there:
+;;   zsh/.zshrc               -> ~/.config/zsh/.zshrc
+;;   tmux/.tmux.conf.local    -> ~/.tmux.conf.local
+;;   swaync/config.json       -> ~/.config/swaync/config.json (a link)
+;;   swaync/style.css         -> ~/.config/swaync/style.css (changed for Guix,
+;;                               see %swaync-style)
+;;   bin/*                    -> ~/.local/bin/* (links, so they stay editable)
+;;   hypr/, waybar/, nvim/... -> ~/.config/... (links, see %linked-configs)
+;;
+;; To add a program: add its name below and run the command again.
+;; To find a name: guix search WORD
+
+(use-modules (gnu home)
+             (gnu home services)
+             (gnu home services desktop)   ;D-Bus
+             (gnu home services shells)    ;zsh
+             (gnu home services sound)     ;PipeWire
+             (gnu packages)
+             ((gnu packages base) #:select (glibc))
+             ((gnu packages bash) #:select (bash-minimal))
+             ((gnu packages elf) #:select (patchelf))
+             ((gnu packages gcc) #:select (gcc))
+             ((gnu packages gl) #:select (mesa))
+             ((gnu packages python) #:select (python))
+             ((gnu packages xdisorg) #:select (hyprpaper))
+             (gnu services)
+             (guix build-system copy)
+             (guix build-system font)
+             (guix build-system trivial)
+             (guix download)
+             (guix gexp)
+             (guix git-download)
+             ((guix licenses) #:prefix license:)
+             (guix packages)
+             (guix utils)
+             (ice-9 ftw)                   ;scandir
+             (srfi srfi-1))                ;filter-map
+
+;;;
+;;; The dotfiles repository
+;;;
+
+;; Two folders above this file: /home/calmy/dots. (The Panasonic home.scm
+;; is one folder higher, in guix/, so it uses one dirname.)
+(define %dots (dirname (dirname (current-source-directory))))
+
+;; A file from the repository, or #f (with a note) if it is not there yet.
+(define (dots-file name relative)
+  (let ((file (string-append %dots "/" relative)))
+    (if (file-exists? file)
+        (local-file (assume-valid-file-name file) name)
+        (begin
+          (format (current-error-port)
+                  "home.scm: ~a is not in the repository yet; skipped~%"
+                  relative)
+          #f))))
+
+;; A link to a file or folder in the repository. The files are not copied
+;; to the store, so you can edit them (and lazy.nvim can write
+;; lazy-lock.json).
+(define (dots-link relative)
+  (computed-file (string-append (basename relative) "-link")
+                 #~(symlink #$(string-append %dots "/" relative) #$output)))
+
+;; Folders in ~/.config that are links to the repository. hypr/hyprland.lua
+;; in the repository is already changed for Guix, so no copying and no
+;; editing is necessary. A name whose folder is not in the repository is
+;; skipped (for example qt6ct, until you add it).
+(define %linked-configs
+  (filter (lambda (name)
+            (file-exists? (string-append %dots "/" name)))
+          '("nvim" "hypr" "waybar" "fuzzel" "alacritty" "fastfetch"
+            "yazi" "helix" "qt6ct")))
+
+;; Every file in bin/ of the repository becomes a link in ~/.local/bin
+;; (nightlight, and any script that you add there later).
+(define %dots-bin
+  (let ((dir (string-append %dots "/bin")))
+    (if (file-exists? dir)
+        (map (lambda (name)
+               (list (string-append ".local/bin/" name)
+                     (dots-link (string-append "bin/" name))))
+             (scandir dir (lambda (name)
+                            (not (string-prefix? "." name)))))
+        '())))
+
+;;;
+;;; Things that Guix does not have
+;;;
+
+;; oh-my-zsh and oh-my-tmux, at fixed versions. To update: change the
+;; commit, run "guix home reconfigure", and copy the new hash from the error.
+(define oh-my-zsh
+  (origin
+    (method git-fetch)
+    (uri (git-reference
+          (url "https://github.com/ohmyzsh/ohmyzsh")
+          (commit "17a2b4c3a5855524ad4772a17ada0dc474e2fdf3")))  ;2026-10-27
+    (file-name "oh-my-zsh-checkout")
+    (sha256
+     (base32 "1pgffrmln711b9afhiqfy3ncfai5pc9lxc3081ya97545mssyllv"))))
+
+(define oh-my-tmux
+  (origin
+    (method git-fetch)
+    (uri (git-reference
+          (url "https://github.com/gpakosz/.tmux")
+          ;; The same .tmux.conf that you used on Debian (2026-08-08).
+          (commit "58a3dcc0d718ec0fa1c0d5a2fddd640a1ad7a5b7")))
+    (file-name "oh-my-tmux-checkout")
+    (sha256
+     (base32 "0zky4qkndrs645xnxh6498zc8yj7y581sg72hh0h7b31a5jxng30"))))
+
+;; Phinger cursors are not in Guix, so this file packages them.
+;; Themes: phinger-cursors-dark, -light, -dark-left, -light-left.
+(define phinger-cursors
+  (package
+    (name "phinger-cursors")
+    (version "2.2")
+    (source (origin
+              (method url-fetch)
+              (uri (string-append "https://github.com/phisch/phinger-cursors"
+                                  "/releases/download/v" version
+                                  "/phinger-cursors-variants.tar.bz2"))
+              (sha256
+               (base32
+                "0mkfbafxskqw02kyn81j9zvhnywglysjg41klfnrrh9q1b1wzm26"))))
+    (build-system copy-build-system)
+    (arguments
+     (list
+      #:phases
+      #~(modify-phases %standard-phases
+          ;; The tarball has four theme folders and no top folder.
+          ;; Unpack it here, without going into a folder.
+          (replace 'unpack
+            (lambda* (#:key source #:allow-other-keys)
+              (invoke "tar" "xf" source))))
+      #:install-plan
+      #~'(("phinger-cursors-dark" "share/icons/")
+          ("phinger-cursors-light" "share/icons/")
+          ("phinger-cursors-dark-left" "share/icons/")
+          ("phinger-cursors-light-left" "share/icons/"))))
+    (home-page "https://github.com/phisch/phinger-cursors")
+    (synopsis "Phinger cursor theme")
+    (description
+     "Phinger is a cursor theme with dark, light and left-handed variants.")
+    (license license:cc-by-sa4.0)))
+
+;; nvim-treesitter needs tree-sitter CLI 0.26.1 or later. Guix has 0.25.3,
+;; so this uses the upstream release binary. The binary expects a normal
+;; Linux file layout, so patchelf points it to Guix's glibc and libgcc.
+(define tree-sitter-cli-bin
+  (package
+    (name "tree-sitter-cli-bin")
+    (version "0.27.1")
+    (source (origin
+              (method url-fetch)
+              (uri (string-append "https://github.com/tree-sitter/tree-sitter"
+                                  "/releases/download/v" version
+                                  "/tree-sitter-linux-x64.gz"))
+              (sha256
+               (base32
+                "0zcbr5hdjd75mn8dyaz43d1nd0sxywsy524pviyq5mm39gwl446h"))))
+    (build-system copy-build-system)
+    (arguments
+     (list
+      #:install-plan #~'(("tree-sitter" "bin/"))
+      #:phases
+      #~(modify-phases %standard-phases
+          (replace 'unpack
+            (lambda* (#:key source #:allow-other-keys)
+              (copy-file source "tree-sitter.gz")
+              (invoke "gzip" "-d" "tree-sitter.gz")
+              (chmod "tree-sitter" #o755)))
+          (add-after 'install 'patch-elf
+            (lambda* (#:key inputs #:allow-other-keys)
+              (let ((bin (string-append #$output "/bin/tree-sitter")))
+                (invoke "patchelf" "--set-interpreter"
+                        (search-input-file inputs "/lib/ld-linux-x86-64.so.2")
+                        bin)
+                (invoke "patchelf" "--set-rpath"
+                        (string-append
+                         (dirname (search-input-file inputs "/lib/libgcc_s.so.1"))
+                         ":"
+                         (dirname (search-input-file inputs "/lib/libc.so.6")))
+                        bin)))))))
+    (native-inputs (list patchelf))
+    (inputs (list glibc (list gcc "lib")))
+    (supported-systems '("x86_64-linux"))
+    (home-page "https://tree-sitter.github.io/")
+    (synopsis "Tree-sitter command-line tool (upstream binary)")
+    (description "This package provides the @command{tree-sitter} command.")
+    (license license:expat)))
+
+;; hyprpaper 0.8 draws with hyprtoolkit. Guix links hyprtoolkit to the libEGL
+;; of libglvnd, but Guix's mesa is built without libglvnd, so that libEGL has
+;; no driver and hyprpaper stops with
+;;   CRIT from hyprtoolkit ]: [GL] eglGetProcAddress(eglCreateImageKHR) failed
+;; Hyprland and hyprlock use mesa's libEGL directly, so they work.
+;; This "hyprpaper" command loads mesa's EGL and GLES first, then starts the
+;; real hyprpaper. Nothing is compiled. Remove it when Guix fixes hyprtoolkit.
+(define hyprpaper-with-mesa
+  (package
+    (name "hyprpaper-with-mesa")
+    (version (package-version hyprpaper))
+    (source #f)
+    (build-system trivial-build-system)
+    (arguments
+     (list
+      #:modules '((guix build utils))
+      #:builder
+      #~(begin
+          (use-modules (guix build utils))
+          (let* ((bin    (string-append #$output "/bin"))
+                 (script (string-append bin "/hyprpaper")))
+            (mkdir-p bin)
+            (call-with-output-file script
+              (lambda (port)
+                (format port "#!~a
+export LD_PRELOAD=\"~a ~a${LD_PRELOAD:+ $LD_PRELOAD}\"
+exec ~a \"$@\"~%"
+                        #$(file-append bash-minimal "/bin/sh")
+                        #$(file-append mesa "/lib/libEGL.so.1")
+                        #$(file-append mesa "/lib/libGLESv2.so.2")
+                        #$(file-append hyprpaper "/bin/hyprpaper"))))
+            (chmod script #o755)))))
+    (home-page (package-home-page hyprpaper))
+    (synopsis "Hyprpaper, started with Mesa's EGL")
+    (description
+     "This package provides a @command{hyprpaper} command that loads Mesa's
+EGL and GLES libraries first, then starts Hyprpaper.")
+    (license (package-license hyprpaper))))
+
+
+;; CaskaydiaCove Nerd Font (Cascadia Code with the Nerd Font icons). Your
+;; swaync style uses "CaskaydiaCove Nerd Font Propo". Fedora had it
+;; (cascadia-code-nf-fonts); Guix does not, so this file packages it.
+(define font-nerd-cascadia-code
+  (package
+    (name "font-nerd-cascadia-code")
+    (version "3.5.1")
+    (source (origin
+              (method url-fetch)
+              (uri (string-append "https://github.com/ryanoasis/nerd-fonts"
+                                  "/releases/download/v" version
+                                  "/CascadiaCode.tar.xz"))
+              (sha256
+               (base32
+                "0sc3m8imzx4ii5vg0m2b9a20njg0bmqi6i9nxsinm17206a8wndf"))))
+    (build-system font-build-system)
+    (arguments
+     (list
+      #:phases
+      #~(modify-phases %standard-phases
+          ;; The tarball has the font files and no top folder.
+          (replace 'unpack
+            (lambda* (#:key source #:allow-other-keys)
+              (invoke "tar" "xf" source))))))
+    (home-page "https://www.nerdfonts.com/")
+    (synopsis "Cascadia Code font with Nerd Font icons (CaskaydiaCove)")
+    (description
+     "This package provides CaskaydiaCove Nerd Font: the Cascadia Code font
+with the Nerd Font icons added, in normal, Mono and Propo forms.")
+    (license license:silofl1.1)))
+
+(define swaynotificationcenter
+  (specification->package "swaynotificationcenter"))
+
+;; Your swaync/style.css starts with
+;;   @import url("file:///etc/xdg/swaync/style.css");
+;; That line loads the default swaync style on Fedora. Guix has no
+;; /etc/xdg/swaync, so on Guix that line loads nothing, and the panel gets
+;; your colors without the base style. This makes a copy of your style.css
+;; that loads the default style from the swaync package instead. The file
+;; in the repository does not change. After you edit it, run
+;; "guix home reconfigure" again (style.css is a copy, not a link).
+(define %swaync-style
+  (let ((file (string-append %dots "/swaync/style.css")))
+    (and (file-exists? file)
+         (computed-file
+          "swaync-style.css"
+          #~(begin
+              (use-modules (ice-9 textual-ports)
+                           (srfi srfi-1))
+              (let* ((default #$(file-append swaynotificationcenter
+                                             "/etc/xdg/swaync/style.css"))
+                     (user (call-with-input-file
+                               #$(local-file (assume-valid-file-name file)
+                                             "swaync-user-style.css")
+                             get-string-all))
+                     ;; All lines except the @import of the Fedora path.
+                     (lines (remove
+                             (lambda (line)
+                               (and (string-prefix? "@import"
+                                                    (string-trim line))
+                                    (string-contains line
+                                                     "swaync/style.css")))
+                             (string-split user #\newline))))
+                (call-with-output-file #$output
+                  (lambda (port)
+                    ;; A newer swaync can keep its default style inside the
+                    ;; program. Then there is no file to import.
+                    (if (file-exists? default)
+                        (format port "@import url(\"file://~a\");~%" default)
+                        (display "/* this swaync has no default style file */\n"
+                                 port))
+                    (display (string-join lines "\n") port)))))))))
+
+;;;
+;;; Programs
+;;;
+
+(define %my-packages
+  '(;; Hyprland and its tools
+    ;; hyprpaper: see hyprpaper-with-mesa above (added below, in packages)
+    "hyprland" "hyprlock" "hypridle" "hyprsunset"
+    "hyprpolkitagent"
+    "xdg-desktop-portal" "xdg-desktop-portal-hyprland" "xdg-desktop-portal-gtk"
+    "waybar" "fuzzel" "swaynotificationcenter" "libnotify"
+    "grim" "slurp" "swappy" "wl-clipboard"
+    "dbus" "xdg-utils" "qt6ct"
+    ;; gsettings, for the dark GTK theme and cursor lines in hyprland.lua
+    "glib:bin" "gsettings-desktop-schemas" "dconf"
+
+    ;; Waybar modules and their click actions: sound, brightness, media,
+    ;; network (nm-connection-editor), Bluetooth (blueman, bluetoothctl)
+    "pipewire" "wireplumber" "pavucontrol"
+    "brightnessctl" "playerctl"
+    "network-manager-applet" "blueman" "bluez"
+    ;; USB drives mount by themselves (udiskie --notify in hyprland.lua)
+    "udiskie"
+
+    ;; GNOME features on Hyprland (the swaync quick settings panel)
+    "wlsunset"               ;bin/nightlight (Super+N, the Night Light button)
+    "gnome-control-center"   ;the Networks, Devices and Settings buttons
+
+    ;; Programs (the Panasonic list)
+    "firefox" "bitwarden-desktop" "alacritty" "btop" "tmux" "neovim"
+    "yazi" "fastfetch" "git" "curl" "wget" "fzf" "ast-grep" "lldb" "helix" "ruff"
+
+    ;; Programs from Fedora
+    "libreoffice" "hunspell-dict-en-us"   ;the dictionary: spelling check
+    "mpv" "krita" "kdenlive"
+    "electrum"               ;Bitcoin wallet. Restore ~/.electrum (INSTALL.md)
+    "torbrowser"             ;the command is "torbrowser"
+    "wine64"                 ;also runs 32-bit Windows programs
+    "steam"                  ;from nonguix, runs in a container (INSTALL.md)
+    "flatpak"                ;for Zen, which Guix does not have (INSTALL.md)
+
+    ;; zsh: plugins, and the programs that your aliases use
+    "zsh-autosuggestions" "zsh-syntax-highlighting"
+    "eza" "bat" "gdb"
+    "ncurses" "perl"   ;clear, tput (zshrc, oh-my-tmux); perl (oh-my-tmux)
+
+    ;; Neovim (LazyVim): search, build tools for treesitter parsers
+    "ripgrep" "fd" "gcc-toolchain" "make" "unzip"
+    ;; Language servers from your lsp.lua. pyright, bashls and ts_ls come
+    ;; from npm (see ../INSTALL.md, F5). nixd and hls are not here.
+    "clang"                  ;clangd
+    "gopls" "sqls" "lua-language-server" "shfmt"
+    "rust" "rust:cargo" "rust-analyzer"   ;rustaceanvim
+    "node"                   ;npm, for the servers above
+    ;; molten-nvim and jupytext.nvim (Python host and Jupyter)
+    "python-pynvim" "python-jupyter-client@7" "python-nbformat"
+    "python-ipykernel@6" "python-jupytext"
+    ;; conjure + swank (your Lisp autocmd), metals (Scala)
+    "sbcl" "openjdk"
+
+    ;; Fonts and icons. Noto Sans CJK shows Japanese text.
+    ;; breeze-icons: the Qt icon theme that Fedora used (breeze-dark).
+    "font-nerd-jetbrains-mono" "font-google-noto" "font-google-noto-emoji"
+    "font-google-noto-sans-cjk" "adwaita-icon-theme" "hicolor-icon-theme"
+    "gnome-themes-extra" "breeze-icons"))
+
+;; Like specifications->packages, but a name that Guix does not have is
+;; skipped with a note, so that one wrong name does not stop the rest.
+;; Read the output of "guix home reconfigure" for "skipped".
+;; (specification->package stops Guix for an unknown name, so first look
+;; for the name with find-best-packages-by-name.)
+(define (specification->name+version spec)
+  ;; "glib:bin" -> "glib" #f, "python-ipykernel@6" -> "python-ipykernel" "6"
+  (let* ((colon (string-index spec #\:))
+         (base  (if colon (substring spec 0 colon) spec))
+         (at    (string-index base #\@)))
+    (if at
+        (values (substring base 0 at) (substring base (+ at 1)))
+        (values base #f))))
+
+(define (specifications->packages/skip specs)
+  (filter-map
+   (lambda (spec)
+     (call-with-values (lambda () (specification->name+version spec))
+       (lambda (name version)
+         (if (null? (find-best-packages-by-name name version))
+             (begin
+               (format (current-error-port)
+                       "home.scm: Guix has no package ~s; skipped~%" spec)
+               #f)
+             (call-with-values
+                 (lambda () (specification->package+output spec))
+               list)))))
+   specs))
+
+;; Your hyprland.lua needs Hyprland 0.55 or later. Print the version.
+(let ((version (package-version (specification->package "hyprland"))))
+  (format (current-error-port) "home.scm: Hyprland ~a~a~%" version
+          (if (version>=? version "0.55")
+              " (reads hyprland.lua)"
+              " -- OLDER THAN 0.55, it does not read hyprland.lua")))
+
+;;;
+;;; Dotfiles
+;;;
+
+(define %zshrc (dots-file "zshrc" "zsh/.zshrc"))
+(define %tmux-local (dots-file "tmux.conf.local" "tmux/.tmux.conf.local"))
+
+;; Files in your home folder.
+(define %home-files
+  (append
+   (filter
+    identity
+    (list
+     ;; oh-my-tmux, the same as on the Panasonic.
+     `(".tmux.conf" ,(file-append oh-my-tmux "/.tmux.conf"))
+     (and %tmux-local `(".tmux.conf.local" ,%tmux-local))
+     ;; options.lua uses ~/.virtualenvs/neovim/bin/python3 as the Python
+     ;; host. Point it to the Python that has pynvim and jupyter_client.
+     `(".virtualenvs/neovim/bin/python3" ,(file-append python "/bin/python3"))))
+   ;; ~/.local/bin/nightlight and the other scripts in bin/.
+   %dots-bin))
+
+;; Files in ~/.config.
+(define %config-files
+  (append
+   (map (lambda (name) (list name (dots-link name)))
+        %linked-configs)
+   (filter
+    identity
+    (list
+     (and (file-exists? (string-append %dots "/swaync/config.json"))
+          `("swaync/config.json" ,(dots-link "swaync/config.json")))
+     (and %swaync-style
+          `("swaync/style.css" ,%swaync-style))))))
+
+(home-environment
+ (packages (cons* phinger-cursors
+                  tree-sitter-cli-bin
+                  python
+                  hyprpaper-with-mesa
+                  font-nerd-cascadia-code
+                  (specifications->packages/skip %my-packages)))
+ (services
+  (list
+   (simple-service
+    'session-variables home-environment-variables-service-type
+    '(;; Where Hyprland and other programs look for cursor themes. Guix's
+      ;; default list does not include the Guix Home profile.
+      ("XCURSOR_PATH"
+       . "$HOME/.local/share/icons:$HOME/.icons:$HOME/.guix-home/profile/share/icons:/run/current-system/profile/share/icons")
+      ;; Flatpak programs (Zen) in the fuzzel menu.
+      ("XDG_DATA_DIRS"
+       . "$XDG_DATA_DIRS:$HOME/.local/share/flatpak/exports/share")))
+   ;; Session D-Bus. PipeWire, the portals and notifications need it.
+   (service home-dbus-service-type)
+   ;; PipeWire + WirePlumber, with PulseAudio support (Waybar sound module).
+   (service home-pipewire-service-type)
+   ;; zsh: oh-my-zsh from Guix, then your .zshrc from the repository.
+   ;; (Hyprland is started by tuigreet, see config.scm.)
+   (service home-zsh-service-type
+            (home-zsh-configuration
+             (zshrc
+              (filter identity
+                      (list (mixed-text-file "oh-my-zsh-path"
+                                             "export ZSH=" oh-my-zsh "\n")
+                            %zshrc)))))
+   (simple-service 'dotfiles home-files-service-type %home-files)
+   (simple-service 'config-files home-xdg-configuration-files-service-type
+                   %config-files))))
diff --git a/guix/gram/installer.scm b/guix/gram/installer.scm
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..3c718c4
--- /dev/null
+++ b/guix/gram/installer.scm
@@ -0,0 +1,19 @@
+;; installer.scm -- the Guix installer, with the full Linux kernel and
+;; firmware from nonguix, so that the gram's Wi-Fi works in the installer.
+;;
+;; Build it on the Panasonic (Guix with nonguix), see INSTALL.md, A4:
+;;   guix system image --image-type=iso9660 ~/dots/guix/gram/installer.scm
+;;
+;; This is smaller than nonguix's own nongnu/system/install.scm: that file
+;; also builds Guix with nonguix inside the image, which can compile for
+;; hours. The install (INSTALL.md, E3) gets nonguix with "guix time-machine",
+;; so the installer does not need it.
+
+(use-modules (gnu)
+             (gnu system install)       ;installation-os
+             (nongnu packages linux))   ;linux, linux-firmware
+
+(operating-system
+  (inherit installation-os)
+  (kernel linux)
+  (firmware (list linux-firmware)))
